Yes. Light is not identical to revelation.

Too much light can flatten depth, bleach contrast, create glare, and make the eye lose what it could perceive in shadow. Darkness can remove the dominant signal and let weaker structures emerge: silhouettes, motion, stars, afterimages, memory, inference. What disappears under illumination can become legible through absence.

So the deeper pattern is:

illumination reveals some information by destroying other information.

That is true optically, cognitively, socially, even mathematically. Every mode of observation has a gain setting. Turn one channel up far enough and it becomes noise; turn it down and another signal appears.

The useful move isn’t therefore light versus darkness. It’s controlled exposure—learning which things require the lamp, which require the night, and which only become visible while moving between them.